```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
bool is_prime(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= sqrt(n); i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
int main() {
    vector<int> primes;
    for (int i = 2; primes.size() < 4; i++) {
        if (is_prime(i) && (i % 10 == 3 || i % 10 == 7)) {
            primes.push_back(i);
        }
    }
    int product = primes[0] * primes[1] * primes[2] * primes[3];
    cout << "The product of the cubes of " << primes[0] << ", " << primes[1] << ", " << primes[2] << ", and " << primes[3] << " is " << product * product * product << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
Here's how the program works:
1. We define a function `is_prime` that checks whether a number is prime or not. This function uses a simple algorithm that checks whether the number is divisible by any number between 2 and its square root.
2. We define a vector `primes` that will store our four consecutive prime numbers.
3. We loop through all numbers starting from 2 until we have found four prime numbers that satisfy the condition of having a units digit of 3 or 7.
4. Once we have found four prime numbers, we calculate the product of their cubes and output the result.
Note that this program assumes that there are at least four consecutive prime numbers that satisfy the given condition. In reality, there may not be any such numbers, or there may be more than one set of four consecutive primes that satisfy the condition. In those cases, the program would need to be modified to handle those scenarios.

