Debit cards are linked to a checking account and allow you to spend only the amount of money that is available in the account. When you use a debit card to make a purchase, the money is immediately deducted from your account. Debit cards typically have lower fees than credit cards, and there is no interest charged on purchases made with a debit card. However, some banks may charge overdraft fees if you spend more than the available balance in your account.
Credit cards, on the other hand, allow you to borrow money from the card issuer up to a certain credit limit. When you use a credit card to make a purchase, the issuer pays the merchant on your behalf, and you must repay the issuer at a later date. Credit cards typically have higher fees than debit cards, including annual fees, late payment fees, and cash advance fees. Interest is also charged on any unpaid balance, and the interest rate can be quite high.
