Wearable technology can be integrated into workplace safety by providing real-time monitoring of workers' vital signs, location, and environmental conditions. This can help identify potential hazards and prevent accidents before they occur.
Here's an example of a Python code that can be used to implement a smart vest that alerts workers of potential hazards and provides real-time tracking of their location within the facility:
```python
import time
class SmartVest:
    def __init__(self, worker_id):
        self.worker_id = worker_id
        self.location = None
        self.temperature = None
        self.heart_rate = None
        self.alerts = []
    def update_location(self, location):
        self.location = location
    def update_temperature(self, temperature):
        self.temperature = temperature
    def update_heart_rate(self, heart_rate):
        self.heart_rate = heart_rate
    def add_alert(self, alert):
        self.alerts.append(alert)
    def get_alerts(self):
        return self.alerts
    def clear_alerts(self):
        self.alerts = []
class Facility:
    def __init__(self):
        self.workers = []
    def add_worker(self, worker):
        self.workers.append(worker)
    def remove_worker(self, worker):
        self.workers.remove(worker)
    def get_workers(self):
        return self.workers
class Sensor:
    def __init__(self, facility):
        self.facility = facility
    def read_location(self, worker):
        # simulate reading location from a sensor
        location = (1, 2, 3)
        worker.update_location(location)
    def read_temperature(self, worker):
        # simulate reading temperature from a sensor
        temperature = 25
        worker.update_temperature(temperature)
    def read_heart_rate(self, worker):
        # simulate reading heart rate from a sensor
        heart_rate = 80
        worker.update_heart_rate(heart_rate)
class AlertSystem:
    def __init__(self, facility):
        self.facility = facility
    def check_temperature(self, worker):
        if worker.temperature > 30:
            alert = "Worker {} is experiencing high temperature".format(worker.worker_id)
            worker.add_alert(alert)
    def check_heart_rate(self, worker):
        if worker.heart_rate > 100:
            alert = "Worker {} has high heart rate".format(worker.worker_id)
            worker.add_alert(alert)
    def check_location(self, worker):
        if worker.location[0] > 10 or worker.location[1] > 10 or worker.location[2] > 10:
            alert = "Worker {} is outside the designated area".format(worker.worker_id)
            worker.add_alert(alert)
    def send_alerts(self):
        for worker in self.facility.get_workers():
            alerts = worker.get_alerts()
            if alerts:
                print("Sending alerts to supervisor for worker {}".format(worker.worker_id))
                for alert in alerts:
                    print(alert)
                worker.clear_alerts()
def main():
    facility = Facility()
    worker1 = SmartVest(1)
    worker2 = SmartVest(2)
    facility.add_worker(worker1)
    facility.add_worker(worker2)
    sensor = Sensor(facility)
    alert_system = AlertSystem(facility)
    while True:
        for worker in facility.get_workers():
            sensor.read_location(worker)
            sensor.read_temperature(worker)
            sensor.read_heart_rate(worker)
            alert_system.check_temperature(worker)
            alert_system.check_heart_rate(worker)
            alert_system.check_location(worker)
        alert_system.send_alerts()
        time.sleep(1)
if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
```
In this code, we have defined a `SmartVest` class that stores information about a worker's location, temperature, heart rate, and alerts. We also have a `Facility` class that stores information about all the workers in the facility. 
We have defined a `Sensor` class that simulates reading location, temperature, and heart rate from sensors. We also have an `AlertSystem` class that checks for potential hazards based on the worker's location, temperature, and heart rate, and adds alerts to the worker's `SmartVest` object.
The main loop of the program reads the sensor data, checks for potential hazards, and sends alerts to the supervisor if any are detected. The loop runs indefinitely until the program is terminated.
This code can be modified to use actual sensors and integrate with a real-time tracking system to provide accurate location data. It can also be extended to include other sensors such as gas detectors, noise sensors, and motion sensors to provide a comprehensive safety monitoring system.

To send an email using the given JSON data and message data type, you can use the following Go code:
```
package main
import (
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
    "net/smtp"
    "strings"
)
type EmailMessage struct {
    To      string
    Subject string
    Body    string
}
func main() {
    // Define the JSON data
    jsonData := `{
        "customer_name": "John Doe",
        "order_number": "12345",
        "product_name": "Widget",
        "issue_description": "The widget arrived broken"
    }`
    // Parse the JSON data into a map
    var data map[string]string
    err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(jsonData), &data)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println("Error parsing JSON data:", err)
        return
    }
    // Define the email message
    message := EmailMessage{
        To:      "recipient@example.com",
        Subject: "Regarding Your Recent Purchase",
        Body:    fmt.Sprintf(`Dear %s,
Thank you for taking the time to reach out to us about your recent purchase. We are sorry to hear that you are not completely satisfied with your %s. Our team takes all customer feedback seriously and we would like to work with you to find a solution.
In order to better assist you, could you please provide us with more information about the issue you are experiencing with your order %s? %s.
Once we receive this information, we will work quickly to resolve the issue and ensure that you are completely satisfied with your purchase. Thank you for choosing our company and we look forward to resolving this issue for you.
Best regards,
[Your Name]`, data["customer_name"], data["product_name"], data["order_number"], data["issue_description"]),
    }
    // Send the email
    err = sendEmail(message)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println("Error sending email:", err)
        return
    }
    fmt.Println("Email sent successfully!")
}
func sendEmail(message EmailMessage) error {
    // Define the SMTP server configuration
    smtpServer := "smtp.gmail.com"
    smtpPort := "587"
    smtpUsername := "your-email@gmail.com"
    smtpPassword := "your-email-password"
    // Define the email message
    msg := "To: " + message.To + "\r\n" +
        "Subject: " + message.Subject + "\r\n" +
        "\r\n" +
        message.Body
    // Connect to the SMTP server
    auth := smtp.PlainAuth("", smtpUsername, smtpPassword, smtpServer)
    conn, err := smtp.Dial(smtpServer + ":" + smtpPort)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    defer conn.Close()
    // Authenticate to the SMTP server
    if err := conn.Auth(auth); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    // Set the sender and recipient
    if err := conn.Mail(smtpUsername); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if err := conn.Rcpt(message.To); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    // Send the email message
    data := strings.NewReader(msg)
    if err := conn.Data(); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if _, err := data.WriteTo(conn); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    return nil
}
```
